{"title":"2025 AIME II Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of vertices of a regular <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />-gon. Find the number of ways to draw <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> segments of equal lengths so that each vertex in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is an endpoint of exactly one of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> segments.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The segments we draw must be of equal length, corresponding to a specific step size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> (number of steps between vertices).\n</p><p>For each step size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, we need to determine if it is possible to form a perfect matching (non-overlapping segments covering all vertices). The number of such perfect matchings depends on the greatest common divisor (gcd) of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> and 24.\n</p><p>When choosing a step size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, the 24-gon is decomposed into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/1/171c557b4c5dc1d2f2bf3504ecf90a96e5c6e297.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(k, 24)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> cycles, each of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/1/7016fc968240823804509afc2bac72fd19485812.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{24}{\\gcd(k, 24)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"84\" height=\"43\" />. For a perfect matching to exist, each cycle must be of even length (since each line segment consists of 2 points, the length of the cycle must be divisible by 2).\n</p><p>For each valid step size (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />):\n</p><p>If the cycle length is 2 (diameters), there is exactly 1 way to match the vertices.\n</p><p>For other even cycle lengths, each cycle contributes a factor of 2 to the number of perfect matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/f/00fab47c0b9d0ce398d03e57d6720129d53434f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e297ce4b6432d4e829ecdce796d113e6d54fb320.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(1, 24) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 24, 2 matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/4/8e44ca2cae6be42148fe1b1fd8b5d55bd6b599bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/6/bd6d8f08a15a2f4aa08a87fef43810b435ed7c84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(2, 24) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 12, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/b/adbe5e0b69a94b87e6e3f4a62abbfaba257982f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^2 = 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/9/7f96a3de68cfb881f4b3cc002ec222bba93f1e16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/8/8b8b8955c6c46e37506bb9211f49bcbeb4efdde4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(3, 24) = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 8, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/5/505e6cf0a34ba67a0715f6ff06c31831fd61532b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^3 = 8)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/4/1b4096f1768d4b1191ccc0386de6b4cc16503a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/d/79d40e394fa045e06a8b68c266627a8dfb3442db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(4, 24) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 6, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c088407a17fcb1f48f288a878f06d13d69102ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^4 = 16)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94be282a752a4f13a21a13c8abbf7c6185434fa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 5$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/c/82c72f676b1f082db94e21595a2d078b18afeceb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(5, 24) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 24, 2 matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/3/5030b8b2e1983047683733deb951a552b42ed896.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 6$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1be7ffb6a9fc27c93d4e03fcd371cf1d1983e5f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(6, 24) = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 4, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/f/6af6fa35b01f9afd8a00f57df1deaae3ba1de660.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^6 = 64)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/9/869588262d13ad049e1639efd917b7eca81d1491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/d/90d2ad8bf015f370f52619d3b43d4f95c553206e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(7, 24) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 24, 2 matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/0/9807ed176ecfd75ce05044e410875fb94d525520.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 8$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d244841f6d9355de26089fc0dac117954e7e2470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(8, 24) = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 3 (invalid, no matchings).\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/c/39cf7f504be23d1bc0ac230ded9a6662f29727e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/274d9ded6ff6fb0068f4bbe61462093df57f7637.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(9, 24) = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 8, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/5/505e6cf0a34ba67a0715f6ff06c31831fd61532b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^3 = 8)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/5/5c593c45953fbca6d0da4893f2126c6f16bf6cf1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12bbf4834c06b70b13af75850d04ed7803e76022.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(10, 24) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 12, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/b/adbe5e0b69a94b87e6e3f4a62abbfaba257982f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^2 = 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"66\" height=\"21\" /> matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/4/084bc95d4a6a09fe562b9de5c6d5f620a04fd84f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/4/9942bdecf1e15e3c045f85c6d2054becb73d38cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(11, 24) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 24, 2 matchings.\n</p><p>(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff55cb14a077c0b7eec45cac1c6710ac2b6f2a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/4/8245990c7f10671c93b7bfc599177c5567e7ac07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(12, 24) = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" />, cycle length 2, 1 matching.\n</p><p>Summing these values: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/a/04a073ee67d0a0ef3b5f4af5bc27be107dca626b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 2 + 64 + 2 + 0 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = \\boxed{113}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"436\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Athmyx\">Athmyx</a>\n</p><p>~ Minor edits by Christian\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/b/30b8e3570268635602228e4518a12e8b93f45e32.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  unitsize(4cm);  int n = 24; pair[] v = new pair[n];  // 1. Generate vertices for(int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {     v[i] = dir(i * 15); // 360 / 24 = 15 degrees }  // 2. Draw background 24-gon draw(polygon(n), dashed + gray(0.8));  // 3. Construct paths manually path p12, p8, p6, p4;  for(int i = 0; i &lt; 12; ++i) { p12 = p12 -- v[(i * 2) % 24]; } for(int i = 0; i &lt; 8;  ++i) { p8  = p8  -- v[(i * 3) % 24]; } for(int i = 0; i &lt; 6;  ++i) { p6  = p6  -- v[(1 + i * 4) % 24]; } for(int i = 0; i &lt; 4;  ++i) { p4  = p4  -- v[(2 + i * 6) % 24]; }  // 4. Draw the remaining polygons draw(p12 -- cycle, blue + 1bp); draw(p8  -- cycle, heavygreen + 1.2bp); draw(p6  -- cycle, red + 1.4bp); draw(p4  -- cycle, orange + 1.6bp);  // 5. Draw dots for(int i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) {     dot(v[i], darkgray + 3bp); }  [/asy]\" width=\"385\" height=\"385\" />\n</p><p>As daunting as it seems, there is actually a very nice way to approach this problem. In the 24-gon, we have a total of 252 diagonals.\n</p><p>The problem simply asks for how many possible diagonals can we use to connect all vertices exactly once so that all such connections are of equal length.\n</p><p>To solve this, consider the 12-gons that are inscribed in the 24-gon. We have two such 12-gons that could exist, ones with vertices on the even indices, and ones with vertices on the odd indices. Every single 12-gon contributes 54 diagonals, all of which are shared by both the 24-gon and 12-gon. This implies that because the 12-gon shares these vertices with the 24-gon, not enough diagonals of that length can actually be formed to conjoin two vertices in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. So, we have to subtract these.\n</p><p>WLOG consider the dodecagon with vertices on odd indices. The octagon with sides on the even vertices of this dodecagon also contribute 20 diagonals that are shared within the 24-gon not apart of the 12-gon particularly. We have to \"overcount\" here because if we were to consider the octagon apart of the 24-gon set instead of the 12-gon set, we do not consider the diagonals of the 12-gon set. So by technicality, not overcounting is undercounting here. Only one such octagon can be formed as otherwise we will overcount if the octagon is on the same odd indices of the dodecagon, as NOW if we remove the dodecagon, the diagonals of the octagon are already considered in the dodecagon. \n</p><p>By now one can see the pattern. Consider the hexagon inscribed within the octagon, and it must have its vertices on the odd indices. This doesn't overcount as they aren't relative to any diagonal part of the dodecagon, they are just inscribed within the octagon. We have 9 diagonals.\n</p><p>Finally, consider the square inscribed in the hexagon, we have 2 diagonals.\n</p><p>Because the two diagonals of the square are equal, the next polygon cannot be inscribed within the square, and therefore the counting ends here.\n</p><p>The number of remaining diagonals can all be used to create sets, that satisfy the problem requirements. The number of ways we can construct such sets is the number of remaining diagonals, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/8/70825a3806f969d2b2a9f59aba4bbd0b97eac98b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$252- 2\\cdot 54 - 20 - 9 - 2 =  \\boxed{113}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"258\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>We do not multiply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76bd3136592ce3207d192249afc597e713bf620c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$54+20+9+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"123\" height=\"15\" /> by 2 because the octagon, hexagon, etc. formed are all uniquely determined not dependent on the dodecagon placement itself, rather on the diagonals of the dodecagon.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/B8FHw94oPYY?si=EKsbbgzuhA5w2G4Q\">2025 AIME II #11</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II\" title=\"2025 AIME II\">2025 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2025 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2025\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">